00:00:03.679 --> 00:00:04.789 align:start position:0%
 
investors  being an entrepreneur is hard having  supportive and caring investors helps  fred wilson how do you write a business  plan for investors  it doesn't matter if you have a business  degree it doesn't matter if you  graduated from university college high  school  or the school of hard knocks it doesn't  matter what your last name is  what your skin color is how old you are  or whether you wear pants or a skirt  i used to be a venture capitalist a vc  people came to me to raise big money for  their businesses  i'm talking 500 000 to 15 million  dollars kind of money  and today i'm going to share with you  the three most important things i look  for  before inviting someone in to see me  99.9 of business plans got rejected  only the 0.1 percent of entrepreneurs  who followed these three  simple rules got invited to a meeting  with us  so if you're thinking about raising  capital or just want an education  as to how the process works you'll want  to pay attention to this one  number one executive summary the  executive summary is the first  thing an investor venture capitalist  angel banker and so on  looks at it's one to three pages at the  beginning of your business plan that  summarizes the whole opportunity  write it last make it important  keep it simple there was an expression  in the vc world that an executive  summary had to pass the grandmother test  meaning that it had to be written in  plain english so that your grandmother  could understand it  most business plans are filled with so  much jargon and technical terms  that investors get lost remember  investors are generalists  not specialists they don't know your  business like you do  it's your job to make it easy for them  to understand the opportunity  if they can't understand how they're  going to make money from your executive  summary  then your entire business plan goes to  the trash can  and that's your fault keep it  simple number two  management team assuming they like your  executive summary the next thing to look  at  is your management team they want to see  what kind of experience you have  have you done this before do you have  any startup experience  are you the right person to actually  execute this idea of yours  one of the biggest problems with most  startup companies is an incomplete  management team  for example you've got a founder who  can't sell and doesn't have a sales  person  or you've got a tech company without a  cto or everybody involved is young and  this is the first thing they're trying  all of those things scream risk for an  investor  to give yourself the best chance at a  yes find people to complement your team  it could be someone who's a partner a  full-time or part-time employee  or even a member on your board of  advisors pro  tip create a board of advisors get  respected people to lend their name to  your company  so it doesn't look like it's just you  for example if nobody who really  understands finances is on your team  that's going to worry an investor get  your accountant to join your board of  advisors to add credibility  and make investors feel more comfortable  number three financials investors want  to know that they're going to get a  return on their investment  banks for example want to know that you  can pay them back and they'll likely  want your house as collateral  angel or vc investors will want to see  that your business can give them an  average annual return of 30 percent on  their investment they want to see that  you can grow  quickly and that someone is likely to  buy you out  they want to be in and out of your  business in six to eight years  can they get that kind of return through  your financials  if they can't they're out also make sure  that you understand  every number on your financial  projections they're going to test your  assumptions and if you stumble  you're going to lose them the next step  you'll need to send an entire business  plan in but those are the three  main sections investors will look at if  you pass a test on those three then the  investor will likely read the rest of  the plan  but would be thinking in the back of his  or her head i'd like to meet this  entrepreneur  and that's what you want that first  meeting where you can show your passion  and get them excited about the  opportunity with you  it's not about having a business degree  you don't need it to write a business  plan  your plan just has to make sense make it  easy for investors to understand your  business  and you're that much closer to getting a  yes from them  believe  you

